Revision history for Perl extension NetAddr::IP

{{$NEXT}}

    Group the IP.pm methods, rewrite its SYNOPSIS, and document netlimit
    (GH#100, step 3 continued). The Methods section was one flat =over of
    forty-eight entries, so a reader looking for how to enumerate hosts
    scanned forty-eight headings. It is now ten =head2 groups, each its
    own =over:

      Constructors           new new6 new_no new_from_aton
                             new_cis new_cis6
      Address and mask       addr mask masklen bits version cidr aton
      Boundaries             network broadcast first last range
      Numeric forms          numeric bigint
      Text forms             short canon full full6 full6m prefix
                             nprefix wildcard
      Containment            contains within is_rfc1918 is_local
      Splitting              split splitref rsplit rsplitref
      Set operations         compact compactref coalesce
      Hosts                  num nth hostenum hostenumref
      Regular expressions    re re6

    That reorders entries, so each block was moved whole and checked to
    have survived verbatim; 48 of 48 did, and the POD item count is
    unchanged at 63. podchecker needs =head2 outside an =over, so each
    group is its own list rather than one list with headings inside it.

    The SYNOPSIS was 113 lines: an import list, every signature, the
    case policy, the FQDN note, Storable, Compact, Coalesce and
    netlimit, all of it mixed together. It is now fifteen pasteable lines
    with results in comments, and the material that was not a synopsis
    moved to its own IMPORT TAGS section:

      print $ip->addr, "\n";         # 192.0.2.1
      print $ip->mask, "\n";         # 255.255.255.0
      print $ip->network, "\n";      # 192.0.2.0/24
      print $ip->broadcast, "\n";    # 192.0.2.255/24
      print "$ip\n";                 # 192.0.2.1/24
      my $next = $ip + 5;            # 192.0.2.6/24
      my @halves = $ip->split(25);   # 192.0.2.0/25, 192.0.2.128/25

    B11, netlimit was documented only inside the SYNOPSIS, which is where
    the code lived too, and it returns the new limit rather than "true on
    success". It has a FUNCTIONS section now, and the values are the
    measured ones:

      netlimit(20)   1048576
      netlimit(16)   65536
      netlimit(24)   16777216
      netlimit(10)   undef, below the default
      netlimit(25)   undef, above the maximum

    B4 through B7 are documented under the constructor, where the
    abbreviated accepted-forms list is, with the mask-argument trap
    spelled out since it is the one that surprises:

      new('10.1')        10.0.0.1/32
      new('10.1', 8)     10.1.0.0/8
      new('10.1.2')      10.1.0.2/32
      new('10.1.2', 24)  10.1.2.0/24

      new('192.0.2.4-7')         192.0.2.4/30
      new('192.0-3.')            192.0.0.0/14
      new('[2001:db8::1]/64')    2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0:1/64
      new('[192.0.2.1]/24')      undef

      new('broadcast')       255.255.255.255/32
      new6('broadcast')      undef, IPv4 keywords only
      new('loopback')        127.0.0.1/8, not /32

    And one correction to what I wrote in the previous commit for this
    step: moving the SYNOPSIS deleted the netlimit sub and its $_netlimit
    default, because the code sat between that heading and INSTALLATION
    and the replacement spanned both. perl -c did not catch it and the
    suite still passed 2535 tests, since nothing calls netlimit. What
    caught it was documenting the function and finding
    NetAddr::IP->can("netlimit") was undef. Restored, in the FUNCTIONS
    section this time. Every edited file was then checked for lost
    definitions:

      IP.pm 31 subs before, 31 after, identical set
      Lite.pm 50/50, Util.pm 7/7, InetBase.pm 29/29,
      UtilPP.pm 28/28, Constants.pm 0/0

    plus the glob aliases, which also match.

    Fix NetAddr::IP and NetAddr::IP::Lite (GH#100, step 3). The two pages
    carry the same object API and have drifted, so they go in one commit
    and the merged text is written once.

    A4, both said splitref "returns undef when bits ... will not fit". It
    croaks:

      NetAddr::IP->new('192.0.2.0/24')->splitref(16);
      # netmask error: overrange or spurious bits

    A6, the IP.pm SYNOPSIS listed inet_any2d and inetanyto6. Neither
    exists; they are inet_any2n and ipanyto6, both of which Util has.

    A11, both new() entries said 'See "STRINGIFICATION" below.' There is
    no such section on either page.

    A12, "The first two methods create..." followed by "The third method
    new_no", after six constructors had been listed. new6FFFF was absent
    from IP.pm's list altogether and works through inheritance, so the
    entry now names it and shows the object it makes:

      NetAddr::IP->new6FFFF('192.0.2.1');
      # 0:0:0:0:0:FFFF:C000:201/128

    A13 is Util.pm, A17 is below. A19, IP.pm's DESCRIPTION promised
    NetAddr::IP "will work with older versions of Perl". META.json says
    5.014, so that is now stated.

    A20 and A22 are the drift between the two pages. IP.pm listed two
    is_local forms where Lite.pm listed four, and Lite.pm's within()
    carried a sentence about comparing addr() that IP.pm lacked. Both are
    merged, verified against the code:

      127.0.0.1        is_local=1
      ::1              is_local=1
      ::127.0.0.1      is_local=1
      ::ffff:127.0.0.1 is_local=1
      ::ffff:10.0.0.1  is_local=0
      128.0.0.1        is_local=0

    The entries are now byte-identical between the two files, which is
    checkable:

      is_local()  identical    mask()  identical     first()  identical
      within()    identical    bits()  identical     last()   identical

    A21, Lite.pm carried the numeric() float-comparison warning and IP.pm
    did not. It is on both now, with the hazard shown rather than
    described, because it is silent:

      $x->numeric == $y->numeric   same    for 2001:db8::1 and ::2
      $x == $y                     different
      $x->bigint == $y->bigint     different

    A23, both new_cis item headings had an unbalanced quote.

    A25, prefix() was documented as including the broadcast address. The
    range starts at first(), not network():

      192.0.2.4/30  ->  192.0.2.5-7
      192.0.2.0/24  ->  192.0.2.
      192.0.2.0/20  ->  192.0.0-15.
      192.0.2.9/32  ->  192.0.2.9

    A27, both pages warned that comparing objects of differing CIDR "may
    produce indeterminate - unexpected results". It is deterministic:
    address first, then the mask as a number. The warning was wrong, and
    the real caveat is that the order is not the one most people mean by
    "bigger", so it says that instead and shows the sort order.

    A28, both listed "a netaddr as returned by 'inet_aton'" among the
    accepted forms without saying it needs the deprecated :aton tag.
    Without it a packed string returns undef, which is now stated. The
    RFC 1884 citations became RFC 4291 s2.2 on both pages.

    A17, the IP.pm NOTES / BUGS section said in the first person that the
    author lacked the resources to get the configure step working on
    Windows and asked for volunteers. It now states the fact and where it
    comes from, which is that header.pl selects pure Perl when $^O
    matches /win/i, and points at README.md for the build steps.

    B1 and B2, the @{} overload and the croaking neg and abs, were
    undocumented on both pages:

      scalar @{$ip}   14 for a /28
      -$ip            cannot negate a NetAddr::IP object
      abs $ip         cannot take the absolute value of a NetAddr::IP object

    B3, the sort order is now given as part of the comparison entry.

    C4, version history inside method text is gone from nth() and num() on
    both pages. Both entries carry measured output instead:

      /32 nth(0) is the address itself, num 1
      /31 nth(0) is the network address, num 2
      /30 nth(0) is the first host, num 2
      /28 num 14

    and the C<:old_nth> tag still restores the old behaviour, verified:

      old_nth 192.0.2.0/30  nth 0 undef, 1..3 the hosts, num 3

    C9, IP.pm's HISTORY section, which said only "See the Changes file",
    is removed and the internal link now points at DEPRECATED.

    Also fixed in passing: "array contest" for "array context" in IP.pm
    and Lite.pm numeric(), a doubled space in Lite.pm bigint(), and the
    C3.02 version note in compact().

    POD only, no code change. Still outstanding from this step: the
    =head2 method groups, the SYNOPSIS rewrite, and B4 through B12 for the
    constructor entry, which is where the accepted-forms list lives and is
    long enough to need more than a correction.

    Fix NetAddr::IP::InetBase and NetAddr::IP::UtilPP (GH#100, step 2,
    continued). The two carry copies of the entries fixed in Util.pm, so
    they go in the same step.

    A7, the InetBase SYNOPSIS said "use NetAddr::IP::Base qw(...)", and
    the module is NetAddr::IP::InetBase, so the line as printed could not
    run. It was also followed by a NetAddr::IP::Util line pasted from
    Util.pm showing Util's tags, which InetBase does not have. Both are
    gone; the SYNOPSIS is now InetBase's own nineteen names with measured
    output, and its four tags are listed with their real contents,
    recovered by importing each into a scratch package:

      :ipv4   fillIPv4 inet_aton inet_ntoa, three names
      :ipv6   eleven names

    The old list for :ipv6 was missing four of those.

    A8, InetBase documented ipv4to6. It is neither defined nor exported
    there; the function belongs to NetAddr::IP::Util, and
    InetBase->can("ipv4to6") is undef. The entry is removed.

    A9, InetBase said lower() "is the default" and Util.pm said upper()
    "is the default", and both were right about themselves and wrong
    about the reader's program. Measured:

      InetBase alone:          ipv6_n2x = 2001:db8:0:0:0:0:0:1
      after InetBase::upper() ipv6_n2x = 2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0:1
      Util loaded:             ipv6_n2x = 2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0:1

    InetBase defaults to lowercase, and loading Util, Lite or NetAddr::IP
    imports :upper for you, so in practice uppercase is the default for
    nearly every program. There is now one section, THE CASE POLICY, that
    says so once, shows the process-wide behaviour where an unrelated
    package importing :lower changes the output for everyone else, and
    points at GH#7 for the open question of what the policy should be.
    ipv6_ntoa and inet_ntop are called out as always lowercase.

    Worth recording, since the old entries implied otherwise: neither
    lower nor upper is importable from either module.

      NetAddr::IP::InetBase->can("lower")   undef
      NetAddr::IP::Util->can("lower")       undef

    They must be called fully qualified, NetAddr::IP::InetBase::upper(),
    and the entries now say so.

    fillIPv4 was documented as converting "127.1 to the long form
    127.0.0.1", which is right, but nothing said what it does with
    anything else, and it is not what a reader would guess. Measured:

      fillIPv4('192.0.2.1')      192.0.2.1
      fillIPv4('192.0.2')        192.0.0.2
      fillIPv4('192.0')          192.0.0.0
      fillIPv4('10')             0.0.0.10
      fillIPv4('example.com')    example.com, unchanged
      fillIPv4('256.1.1.1')      undef

    The argument is text, not a packed address, which is the trap: a
    packed string does not match, so it comes back unchanged. It is also
    the BSD inet_aton convention and not RFC 791, which is the note
    table D asks for, and which belongs here rather than on inet_aton.

    A14, the missing paren in the hasbits example was in this file too,
    at UtilPP.pm:111, so it is fixed in both.

    A15, the UtilPP EXPORT_OK list ended with "threads", which is not a
    function of that module nor of NetAddr::IP::Util; it never existed.
    Removed, and the list now cross references NetAddr::IP::Util instead
    of being a bare list of eighteen names.

    A18's rfc1844 typo was here as well as in Util.pm, and the two rfc1884
    citations became RFC 4291 s2.2.

    UtilPP gets a DESCRIPTION, which it did not have beyond one sentence.
    It explains when the module is selected, that the two implementations
    are meant to be interchangeable and agree down to the error message
    wording, and that README.md carries the build steps, so no page
    discusses building.

    INSTALLATION is removed from InetBase too, per C3.

    A new section on InetBase, SOCKET6 SUBSTITUTION, names the three
    symbols that come from Socket6 when it is installed, says the choice
    is made at load time, and points at GH#99 and GH#21 for where the two
    paths disagree.

    POD only, no code change. The grouped =head2 sections the issue asks
    for across all three function pages, and the table D note about
    inet_aton short forms, are still outstanding.

    Fix NetAddr::IP::Util (GH#100, step 2 of the POD overhaul). The
    SYNOPSIS was a 118 line import list followed by every signature, a
    table of contents rather than a synopsis; it is now about forty lines
    that a reader can paste, and every value in it is the measured output
    rather than a placeholder. INSTALLATION is removed, per the rules,
    and README.md keeps the build steps. The EXAMPLES section did not
    compile at all and now runs on both builds.

    The four defects the issue found in the examples, and what each was:

      $addr = addconst($addr,$const)      scalar context stores the
                                          carry, not the sum
      return ($network, $broadcast)       $broadcast never declared,
                                          the local is $bcast
      my $addr = $nip->{addr}             missing semicolon
      my $lastip = $ip-copy()             not Perl; stringification
                                          copies, "$ip"

    plus an unbalanced paren in (undef, $ip->{addr} = addconst(. The wrap
    is now detected with "$ip" lt $before, since the comparison is on the
    string forms rather than the overloaded operators, and the list
    context form of addconst takes the second element. The loop is shown
    wrapping a /30, so the branch is actually exercised:

      step 3: 192.0.2.252/30   wrapped: 1

    A1, shiftleft with no count. Util.pm said "a single shift is
    performed if $n is not specified" and UtilPP.pm said it returns the
    input. UtilPP was right and both builds agree:

      shiftleft($bits128) eq $bits128      yes, on XS and pure Perl

    Both entries now say so, with an example, and note that $n above
    $MAX_SHIFTLEFT croaks.

    A2, naip_gethostbyname documented IPv4 answers as ::FFFF:FFFF:d.d.d.d,
    which is not an address. The answer for 127.0.0.1 is:

      0:0:0:0:0:FFFF:7F00:1

    so the form is ::FFFF:d.d.d.d, the RFC 4291 s2.5.5.2 mapped prefix,
    which is what the function actually produces on both builds.

    A3, three entries wrote ::ffff::d.d.d.d, which has one colon too
    many. Corrected to ::ffff:d.d.d.d, and the isNewIPv4 and isAnyIPv4
    entries now name the prefixes they test, s2.5.5.2 and the union of
    s2.5.5.1 with it.

    A14, "if (hasbits($bits128 & $mask128) {" was missing its closing
    paren here and in UtilPP.pm.

    A16, the EXPORT_OK list omitted seven names that are in @EXPORT_OK:
    AF_INET, AF_INET6, bin2bcdn, bcdn2txt, bcdn2bin, simple_pack and
    comp128. All seven are now listed, the last five under a heading that
    says they are exported for the test suite and are not stable API,
    which is what the code comments about comp128 said all along. The
    import tags are now listed with their real contents, measured by
    importing each into a scratch package, which turned up that :ipv6 is
    :inet without the two IPv4 names and nothing else, and that the
    original list for it was missing four.

    A18, inet_n2ad was documented as returning x:x:x:x:x:x:ddd.ddd.ddd.dd,
    which has a truncated octet. The real form is x:x:x:x:x:x:ddd.ddd.ddd.ddd,
    and inet_n2ad(2001:db8::1) is 2001:DB8:0:0:0:0:0.0.0.1. Two rfc1844
    typos became RFC 4291 s2.2.

    A24, "=item $ipv6naddr = inet_4map6($netaddr or $ipv6naddr" was
    missing its closing paren in the item heading.

    B9, the :noSock6 import tag, which forces havegethostbyname2 to
    report false so the resolver fallback can be tested on a host that
    has Socket6, was undocumented. It has its own section now, labelled
    a test hook. C7's point that there is no single place listing the
    tags that change behaviour is partly answered; the case tags live in
    NetAddr::IP::InetBase and are cross referenced.

    B10, the split between the functions that return undef on bad input
    and the ones that croak on a wrong length, now stated in the
    entries: ipv6_aton, inet_aton and inet_4map6 return undef, while
    hasbits and isIPv4 croak with "Bad arg length ... length is N,
    should be 128". B11's netlimit is a NetAddr::IP function rather than
    one of these, and is left to step 3.

    The RFC 1884 citations become RFC 4291 s2.2 for the accepted forms
    and RFC 5952 s4 for the output, with packzeros documented against
    s4.2.1, s4.2.2 and s4.2.3 separately and an example for each,
    including that a single zero group is never shortened. Table D's
    note that inet_aton short forms are the BSD convention rather than
    RFC 791 is still outstanding; inet_aton('127.1') is 127.0.0.1 and
    inet_aton('192.168.1') is 192.168.0.1, and inet_aton croaks on
    '0x7f.1', so it belongs with a rewritten inet_aton entry.

    Five names for one concept were unified to $bits128 for a 16 byte
    string and $ipv6naddr for an address, per C5, though the full sweep
    across all three function pages is part of finishing this step.

    POD only, no code change. InetBase.pm and UtilPP.pm carry copies of
    several of these entries and are corrected in the commit that
    follows.

    Fix NetAddr::IP::Constants (GH#100, step 1 of the POD overhaul).
    Unindent the item bodies, which rendered as verbatim blocks under
    pod2text, group the constants by the RFC they come from, and drop the
    claim that importing one "ensures that each constant resolves at
    compile time".

    That claim is false and the opposite is worth saying plainly, since
    the constants are read in the hot path of the 128 bit arithmetic.
    They are our variables, so an assignment writes through to the
    module's own copy, which is what the rest of the distribution reads:

      use NetAddr::IP::Constants qw($IPV6_BITS);
      print "$IPV6_BITS\n";                             # 128
      $IPV6_BITS = 999;
      print "$IPV6_BITS\n";                             # 999
      print "$NetAddr::IP::Constants::IPV6_BITS\n";     # 999

    A caller who has already declared a name the import wants is not
    overwritten, because Exporter only installs the alias when the name
    is free, so overriding is reachable and order dependent. InetBase,
    Util and UtilPP each import these and read their own copy, so a
    caller's reassignment changes nothing inside the library. Documented
    as reachable but unsupported rather than as a supported feature.

    One more correction the issue did not list. $MAX_BCD_DIGITS was
    documented as "40 (i.e. ceil(128 * log10(2)))", and that ceiling is
    39, not 40:

      ceil(128*log10(2)) = 39      decimal digits in 2**128 = 39
      bin2bcd of all ones        = 39 digits, unpadded

    40 is right for a different reason. BCD holds one digit per nibble,
    and a packed string has to be a whole number of bytes, so 39 nibbles
    round up to 40, which is the 20 bytes $PACKED_BCD_BYTES holds, and
    simple_pack is what pads to 40 before pack("H40"). The entry now
    gives that reasoning instead of the false equivalence.

    $IPV4_OFFSET gained the two section numbers it covers, RFC 4291
    s2.5.5.1 for the deprecated compatible prefix and s2.5.5.2 for the
    mapped prefix, since both sit at that offset and the entry had only
    the umbrella citation. Table D's note that inet_aton short forms
    such as 127.1 are the BSD convention and not RFC 791 is left for the
    Util.pm commit that rewrites the function entries.

    POD only, no code change. Every example run on both builds.

    Narrow what canon() claims to follow, and document what it does with
    an IPv4 address in the low 32 bits (GH#98). The POD said ipV6 output
    is "as per RFC5952", which overclaims. Sections 4.1 to 4.3 hold:
    leading zeros dropped, the longest run of zero groups shortened to
    ::, the first of two equal runs shortened, lowercase, and canon is
    the lower case of short() on every address tried. Section 5, the
    mixed form for an embedded IPv4 address, is not applied:

      canon ->new('::ffff:192.0.2.1')   ::ffff:c000:201
      canon ->new6FFFF('192.0.2.1')     ::ffff:c000:201
      canon ->new6('1.2.3.4')           ::102:304

    Deferred to a future decision, GH#98, and this entry takes neither
    path. Mixed notation for the mapped prefix alone is the narrowest
    reading of section 5, the one prefix that is both well known and
    current, and is what Python does; the test is isNewIPv4, which is
    what distinguishes the mapped prefix from the compatible one, since
    isIPv4 is also true of ::1 and its mixed form would be ::0.0.0.1.
    It would change canon for mapped addresses and nothing else, leave
    addr, short, full and stringification in hex since none of them
    claims the RFC, and stop canon and short agreeing for those
    addresses, which the POD would then have to say. Mixed notation for
    the compatible prefix as well would agree with glibc, and therefore
    with ipv6_ntoa where Socket6 binds libc, but it would make canon
    stop agreeing with the ::102:304 that new6 documents, and RFC 4291
    deprecates the form. Either way t/114-canon.t gains the mapped cases
    and keeps ::1 and :: unchanged, and anyone comparing stored canon
    strings of mapped addresses sees a new value, so this belongs in the
    series that already changed re, re6 and new(undef).

    A comment at canon() records the section 5 omission where it happens
    and points at GH#98.

    Document that ipv6_ntoa and inet_ntop render an address with an IPv4
    address in the low 32 bits differently depending on Socket6
    (GH#99). inet_ntop is bound to the platform's libc when Socket6 is
    installed and to the module's own _inet_ntop otherwise, and Socket6
    is a recommendation rather than a requirement. Two hosts differing
    only in that one optional module therefore render
    ::ffff:192.0.2.1 as ::ffff:192.0.2.1 and as ::ffff:c000:201. The
    POD said these functions "come from the Socket6 library if it is
    present on this host" without saying what follows from it.

    The table now in the POD is measured, not reasoned. Over 8,040
    addresses, a structured set plus random ones weighted towards zero
    groups, every disagreement between libc and the fallback fell in the
    mapped prefix ::ffff:0:0/96 or the deprecated compatible prefix
    ::/96, and nowhere else. Zero run compression, which of two equal
    runs is shortened, single zero groups, leading zeros and case agree
    throughout, including on 2001:0:0:1:0:0:0:1, where both shorten the
    second run.

    Also correct "takes and IP address" to "an" in the inet_ntop entry.

    POD only. No method of NetAddr::IP or NetAddr::IP::Lite calls either
    function; stringification goes through ipv6_n2x and packzeros, which
    are pure Perl on every host.

    Deferred to a future decision, GH#99. Two ways out, and this entry
    takes neither. Bind inet_ntop to the module's own implementation
    always, and teach the fallback the mixed form for the mapped prefix.
    That makes the text the same on every host, defined by this module
    rather than by whichever libc is underneath, and stops a libc
    rendering it differently from breaking the suite, which is what GH#33
    was. The cost is that on a host with Socket6 today ::192.0.2.1 comes
    back as ::c000:201, so it moves output for existing callers. Or keep
    libc and teach the fallback to match, which moves nothing today but
    leaves the text defined by the host's libc, leaves the GH#33 class of
    failure in place, and forces the compatible prefix to glibc's
    rendering. That is the opposite of Path B of GH#98, so canon and
    ipv6_ntoa would disagree on ::192.0.2.1 unless GH#98 takes the same
    path. Whichever is chosen, the four tests that pin these functions
    should gain the mapped and compatible cases with one expected value
    each, true with and without Socket6.

    A comment at the fallback binding in InetBase.pm now records the
    divergence where the choice is made, and points at GH#99 and GH#21.

    Drop docs/rfc1884.txt (GH#97). The full text of RFC 1884, "IP
    Version 6 Addressing Architecture", December 1995, shipped with
    4.001 in August 2006 and moved to docs/ by 4.004 a fortnight
    later, and nothing has touched it since. It is 37,854 bytes, 3.98%
    of the unpacked distribution, and the only thing under docs/.

    Nothing read it. No POD links to it, no test opened it, no code
    names the path, and the only reference to docs/ anywhere in the
    tree was the skip line in dist.ini that existed to keep the EOL
    test away from it. The generated t/author-eol.t lists 185 files and
    none under docs/, so removing the file and the skip line leaves
    that test at the same 185 files.

    RFC 1884 was obsoleted by RFC 2373 in July 1998, which RFC 3513
    replaced in April 2003, which RFC 4291 replaced in February 2006.
    The addressing architecture is RFC 4291, and the text
    representation rules that :lower and canon() follow are RFC 5952
    from August 2010. Shipping the 1995 text beside code that
    implements the 2006 rules invites a reader to take it for the
    reference.

    The ten mentions of rfc1884 in the POD name the specification, not
    the file, and are unchanged here. They are being replaced with RFC
    4291 and RFC 5952 citations in the POD work.

    The text remains at https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1884 for
    anyone who wants the history.

    Give the shipped Makefile.PL a clean and realclean list again
    (GH#96). The checkout Makefile.PL has listed the generated files
    since 4.079, but header.pl has not, so the shipped one has had no
    list at all since 0da215c, and six files survived make realclean:
    lib/NetAddr/IP/Util.c, lib/NetAddr/IP/Util_IS.pm, xs/config.h,
    xs/config.log, xs/config.status and xs/localperl.h. Pure Perl left
    Util_IS.pm behind.

    The stale xs/config.h was the part that mattered. Configure runs
    only when that file is missing, so the next perl Makefile.PL in the
    same tree reused the old results and never probed again, whatever
    had changed in between.

    Restore the 4.079 split: make clean removes what Makefile.PL
    rewrites on every run, which is Util_IS.pm in both builds and
    localperl.h in the XS build, and make realclean removes the
    configure results. make clean moves the Makefile aside, so
    perl Makefile.PL runs again before the next build and puts those
    three back; the configure results survive until realclean.

    That required dropping the sub clean override from the footer,
    which kept lib/NetAddr/IP/Util.c out of make clean on the grounds
    that "we need this file tracked in git for CPAN distribution". It
    stopped being tracked at 335f5eb, which untracked it, and 4.080_03
    does not ship it, so the override was protecting nothing. The file
    is not needed in the tree either: the postamble regenerates it from
    xs/Util.xs through xsubpp, and a make clean followed by
    perl Makefile.PL and make builds and tests clean.

    The checkout Makefile.PL loses xs/config.h, xs/config.log and
    xs/config.status from its clean list for the same reason, so the
    two copies do not disagree about what a clean build reuses.

    Verified: XS build then make realclean leaves none of the six;
    pure Perl then make realclean leaves none; after make clean,
    Util.c, Util_IS.pm and localperl.h are gone and the three configure
    results are kept; Util.c comes back on the next make, and the next
    perl Makefile.PL --xs after a realclean probes again. Both builds
    161 files and 2535 tests, all pass.

    Correct the :nofqdn example in NetAddr::IP's POD (GH#92). The
    FQDN paragraph under new() told the reader to load NetAddr::IP::Lite,
    which works, since the flag lives there and both modules read it, but
    every other tag example on that page imports through NetAddr::IP and
    a reader of the NetAddr::IP page has no reason to load Lite. Both
    forms set the same $NetAddr::IP::Lite::NoFQDN, checked here, and
    t/124-nofqdn.t already covers the tag arriving through NetAddr::IP.
    POD only.

    Fix LIBS from configure reaching ExtUtils::MakeMaker by the wrong
    name (GH#95). header.pl read xs/config.h and then replaced
    @mm_args[-1], which is depend's hash ref rather than LIBS', so the
    libraries turned depend into an array ref, LIBS stayed empty, and
    EUMM died before writing a Makefile:
        WARNING: depend takes a HASH reference not a ARRAY reference
        Not a HASH reference at ExtUtils/MakeMaker.pm line 837
    Any library configure had to add, which is libnsl or libsocket on
    illumos and older Solaris, was enough to do it. config.h is only
    rewritten when it is missing, so the tree then stayed broken until it
    was deleted by hand. Read the file first and pass the libraries by
    name now.

    Stop emitting the xs/config.h make rule, and stop referring to $begin
    to do it (GH#93). The rule ran xs/configure, a script this
    distribution has not shipped since 4.069, so it could never succeed;
    it only stayed out of the Makefile by accident, because the footer
    referred to $main::begin, which is the package variable of a
    different thing and is always unset. Every build warned
        Name "main::begin" used only once: possible typo
        Use of uninitialized value $begin in concatenation (.) or string
    and then carried on. No perl makes those fatal. The generated
    Makefile.PL enables warnings with a plain "use warnings", which
    [MakeMaker::Awesome] puts there, so both messages printed, the
    Makefile was written and the suite passed, on every perl in the
    matrix. Only a caller that imposes fatal warnings on the whole
    process, as PERL5OPT=-Mwarnings=FATAL does, turns the first one into
    a build that stops.
    Remove the reference and the warnings go; remove the rule and the
    generated Makefile is byte for byte what it was before, and a
    missing xs/config.h stops the build with make's own
        No rule to make target 'xs/config.h', needed by 'Util.c'
    rather than a failed script. The depend entry still lists it as a
    prerequisite. Makefile.PL itself probes configure.gcc, then
    configure.cc, then falls back to pure Perl before writing anything,
    which a make rule cannot repeat.

    Re-enable the Windows test job, pure Perl only, so the runner needs
    no C toolchain. It had been disabled long enough that the reason was
    forgotten, and the reason was three separate things stacked on top of
    each other:
        - the job ran under a POSIX shell, where "perl" is the Cygwin perl
          that Git Bash puts on PATH, and it has no ExtUtils::MakeMaker, so
          the installer reported a core module as missing
        - the flavour of make ExtUtils::MakeMaker writes a Makefile in
          depends on the perl, 5.24 saying "dmake-style" and 5.26 saying
          "gmake-style", and neither make works across the matrix
        - the dependency install did not request the develop phase, which
          the author tests in the distribution need
    It now installs through cpm via the setup action, which also gets all
    five author tests' prerequisites, and asks for gmake explicitly with
    MAKE=gmake so the Makefile flavour does not vary with the perl.

    The Windows job does not set AUTHOR_TESTING, unlike the others. On
    that platform the setup action installs dependencies into
    local/lib/perl5 inside the project directory, and Test::EOF walks the
    working directory, so the check was reading third-party modules and
    failing on Data::Binary's trailing newlines. Test::EOF takes no file
    list and no finder, so it cannot be pointed elsewhere. The five
    author tests skip themselves without the variable. The four release
    tests do not, and still run; they need the develop phase, which is
    still installed, and they pass on Windows.

    Skip local/ in the other line-ending check as well, the one built on
    the EOLFiles finder, for the same reason. Both checks walk the tree
    and neither can be given a file list from dist.ini.

    Verified on Windows, pure Perl, perl 5.24: 160 files and 3676 tests
    with the one failure above, and 2531 with it gone and the author
    tests skipped. t/013-util-binet_pton.t and
    t/025-util-inet_pton.t, which hard-code the output of inet_ntop, both
    pass, so the difference in GH#33 is glibc against BSD and does not
    reach Windows. GH#33 is closed as unreproduced: all forty macOS jobs
    pass, ten perls from 5.24 across four images, with Socket6 built
    into each so libc, not the pure Perl fallback, is under test. The
    tests still assert the rendered string, so a libc rendering these
    three addresses differently would fail them again.

    Stop the SYNOPSIS of both modules importing behaviour-changing tags
    (GH#91). A SYNOPSIS invites pasting, and both lists pulled in every
    tag the module has. Pasted as written, NetAddr::IP's list warned
    twice, for :aton and :rfc3021, and NetAddr::IP::Lite's warned
    once, for :aton. Under "use warnings FATAL => 'all'" both died at
    compile time, in a program that had only asked for Compact and
    Zeros.

    Worse, the tags change behaviour for the whole program rather than
    returning something. A paste of the NetAddr::IP list also switched
    on the legacy Storable and nth() behaviour, and its :lower did
    nothing at all, because import() handles :lower before :upper, so
    :upper wins whichever order they appear in.

    Each list now names functions only. NetAddr::IP points at where
    the tags are documented: :lower, :upper, :old_storable and
    :nofqdn each have a one-line example where they apply, and :aton
    and :rfc3021 are under DEPRECATED. Lite.pm is the smaller module
    and gets no sentence, and its EXPORT_OK drops the tags to match
    NetAddr::IP's, which lists functions only.

    The tags themselves are unchanged and still import as before; this
    is POD only. No test asserted the SYNOPSIS list, and none needed
    changing.

    Verified that each list now loads clean under both plain and FATAL
    warnings, that all twelve tags across the two modules still import,
    and podchecker is clean on both files.

4.080_03  2026-09-30 23:07:41+10:00 Australia/Sydney (TRIAL RELEASE)

    Reformat the test files with perltidy's default profile. Nine of
    them were indented with tabs while the other 140 used spaces, and
    .editorconfig asks for four columns of spaces, so t/ is now
    internally consistent for the first time. Whitespace is the only
    thing that moved, except for four semicolons perltidy deletes by
    default, being ones that are simply empty statements: the one after
    "sub BASE_LEN () { 7 };" in t/043, and one standing before a closing
    brace in each of t/087, t/096 and t/097. None of them changes what
    the code does.

    Tabs survive in six files, inside comments that use them to line up
    columns in a table of test vectors. perltidy does not rewrite comment
    text, which is the right call, so those stay.

    Checked by stripping all whitespace from each file before and after,
    which is the only guarantee perltidy actually makes, so those four
    semicolons are the whole of the difference. The suite passes either
    way: 160 files and 2927 tests from dzil test --all, and 160 files
    and 2531 tests in each of the XS and pure Perl built distributions.

    No .perltidyrc is checked in, so this records the result rather than
    the settings that produced it, and nothing stops the files drifting
    apart again.

    Name an undefined packed argument in the error, instead of warning
    first and then reporting a length of zero. The guards read their
    argument with length() and then did arithmetic or a comparison on
    the result, so an undef reached an operator first and perl warned
    about a value inside the library, at a line the caller cannot act
    on, before the check that names the function could run:
        inet_ntoa(undef)    Use of uninitialized value in numeric eq
                            (==), at InetBase.pm line 332
                          Bad arg length for ...::inet_ntoa, length is
                          should be 4
        hasbits(undef)      Use of uninitialized value in numeric ne
                            (!=), at UtilPP.pm line 137
                          Bad arg length for ...::hasbits, length is 0,
                          should be 128
    Both halves were wrong. The zero described a length nobody passed,
    and inet_ntoa interpolated an empty string because length(undef) is
    the empty string, so its message named the function and the expected
    width but no length at all. The message now says the length is
    undefined, and nothing warns on the way to it.

    Fixed in both implementations, which have to agree: NetAddr::IP::Util
    is native by default and falls back to UtilPP, and the XS half got
    there differently. It declares its arguments as SV *, and SvPV
    turned an undefined argument into an empty string with a length of
    zero without complaining, so it reported the same misleading zero
    and perl's "Use of uninitialized value in subroutine entry" warning
    fired from the caller's line. Each XS function now tests SvOK
    before it calls SvPV.

    inet_ntoa and ipv6_ntoa are pure perl with no XS counterpart, so
    they only needed the guard. ipv6_ntoa was also handing an undefined
    argument to Socket6::inet_ntop, which reported a length of 0 for
    something that had none.

    A wrong but defined length is still measured and reported as before,
    so "ab" still gives 2 and not undefined.

    Fix addition and subtraction accepting a constant that is not a
    number (GH#89). The guard compared $const == int($const), and perl
    numifies both sides first, so a string passed as its leading digits
    or as zero:
        - '5abc' added or subtracted 5
        - '0x10' and '0b11' added 0, which reads as no change at all
    looks_like_number now runs ahead of the guard, and subtraction
    negates only what is a number so the guard sees the original
    otherwise. Hex, binary, 0o and empty strings are rejected. Padded
    and exponent strings, and a Math::BigInt constant, are unaffected.
    The POD in both IP.pm and Lite.pm said only that the constant must
    be an integer, and now says a non-numeric string croaks.

    Keep the documented behaviour for a missing or zero constant, which
    returns a copy. Nothing tested it before, so the new tests pin it:
    undef and the empty string still return a copy for both operators,
    where subtraction previously died inside the negation and addition
    returned a copy.

    Fix both SYNOPSIS use lists, which carried their annotations as qw()
    members, so each module tried to import a symbol named DEPRECATED and
    Lite.pm one named ! as well (GH#83)

    Consolidate the scattered deprecation notes in IP.pm and Lite.pm into a
    DEPRECATED section under EXPORT_OK, and state there that everything
    listed is going in version 5:
        - the promise was only ever attached to a markdown ###### banner in
          a POD verbatim block, and in Lite.pm that sat 58 lines into the
          new_cis6 item body
        - :rfc3021 said only "a future version", and new_cis and new_cis6
          carried no removal version at all
        - :rfc3021, new_cis and new_cis6 are now covered by the promise
    Each item names its replacement. :old_nth is left out, being a
    compatibility switch that has never been marked deprecated and has no
    replacement.

    Warn in the deprecated category when :aton, new_cis or new_cis6 is
    used, naming the replacement in the warning (GH#49). None of the three
    warned before, though all were marked deprecated:
        - :aton warns on import in both IP.pm and Lite.pm
        - new_cis and new_cis6 warn on call; they are defined once in
          Lite.pm and reached from IP.pm via AUTOLOAD
    Behaviour of all three is unchanged, except under use warnings
    FATAL => 'all', where the new warning dies; no warnings 'deprecated'
    silences it. For an import like :aton that silence has to come
    before the use, since the import runs at BEGIN time.

    Require Math::BigInt 1.99, the last release before perl 5.14 (GH#44)

    Remove duplicate NAME section from the Constants.pm POD

    Adjust Constants.pm POD: name each constant with its sigil and
    indent descriptions consistently

    Convert SEE ALSO sections to L<...> links and drop the (3) suffixes

    Uniform code formatting across lib/, following UtilPP.pm:
        - 4 spaces per indent level, no tabs
        - space after commas, my (...) rather than my(...)
        - =item rather than =item *
        - else and elsif on their own line, not cuddled to the brace
    POD is unchanged by the reindent; verbatim examples keep their
    two-space first level.

    Fix {1,3} regex quantifiers broken by a stray space, which had
    disabled the mixed hex/dot-quad branch of InetBase::ipv6_aton and
    the masklen check in coalesce

    Write the shipped Makefile.PL source as inc/MakeMaker/header.pl and
    reapply the style pass there, so the tarball no longer ships the
    old use vars, indent and =item * forms (GH#85)

    Drop AutoLoader from the prerequisites; nothing loads it since GH#70

    Drop vars from the prerequisites; generated Util_IS.pm now declares
    its version with our $VERSION

    Remove the Math::BigInt emulation switch from Lite.pm (GH#84):
        - _force_bi_emu and $no_mbi_emu are gone, and _loadMBI always
          runs the require, so bigint() no longer croaks on a host that
          has Math::BigInt installed
        - drop the stale note on the three known Math::BigInt flavors

    Document the numeric() comparison hazard in the Lite.pm POD: an
    ipV6 value has more digits than a Perl number holds, so ==, <=> and
    sort treat distinct addresses as equal (GH#84)

